[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence gathered. Let me compile findings:

- BGP sessions: both up, stable (AS1↔AS2)
- Router1 RIB: `B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2` ✓
- Router2 RIB: `B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1` ✓
- pc1 gateway: 195.11.14.1 (router1:eth1) ✓
- pc2 gateway: 200.1.1.1 (router2:eth1) ✓
- ip_forward: 1 on both routers ✓
- iptables: all ACCEPT, no NAT on data path ✓
- rp_filter: 0 (disabled) on all interfaces ✓
- Ping pc1→pc2: **0% loss** ✓
- Ping pc2→pc1: **0% loss** ✓
- Traceroute both ways: correct 2-hop paths ✓

The re-advertising anomaly (both routers re-advertise learned eBGP routes back) is normal BGP propagation behavior, correctly suppressed by AS-PATH loop prevention (confirmed in logs: `DENIED due to: as-path contains our own AS`).

